{"kBP":{"title":"Backprop derivation","mc":"#15803d","body":"<div class=\"m-meta\"><p class=\"role\">Apply the chain rule layer by layer backwards; one backward pass gives every parameter’s gradient.</p><div class=\"where\">📍 Location: <a data-go=\"T1\">App overview</a> › <a data-go=\"T2\">Agent</a> › <a data-go=\"T3\">LLM</a> › <a data-go=\"N\">Neural Network Basics</a> › <a data-go=\"K5\">Math · Derivations &amp; proofs</a> › <b>Backprop derivation</b></div><span class=\"status no\">📐 Math foundation: not a component, but the rule behind the computation</span></div><div class=\"tryit\"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class=\"btn primary\" data-jump=\"labGradCheck\" data-lv=\"K5\">🧪 Backprop vs numerical gradient (Derivations)</button></div><div class=\"m-tabs\" role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Card sections\"><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t0\" aria-controls=\"mt@p0\" aria-selected=\"true\" tabindex=\"0\">🎯 What it does</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t1\" aria-controls=\"mt@p1\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">▶ How to use</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t2\" aria-controls=\"mt@p2\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">🧠 Why</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t3\" aria-controls=\"mt@p3\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p0\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t0\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"sec\"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>Two formulas per layer: <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">⊤</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial L/\\partial W=\\delta\\,x^{\\top}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">W</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0379em;\">δ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">⊤</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">⊤</mi></msup><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial L/\\partial x=W^{\\top}\\delta</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">W</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">⊤</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0379em;\">δ</span></span></span></span>.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p1\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t1\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec\" style=\"background:#f0f9ff\"><h4>▶ How to use it</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>Store each layer’s input in the forward pass and reuse it in the backward pass.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p2\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t2\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec why\"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>The loss is one number and there are many parameters: going backwards from the output computes each intermediate result once.</p></div><div class=\"sec eff\"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>The backward pass does two matrix multiplications per layer versus one forward, so a training step costs about 3× a forward pass.</p></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p3\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t3\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><p class=\"lim\">⚠️ Limits / notes: Forward intermediates must be kept, so memory grows with depth and sequence length.</p></div><!--m2pi--><div class=\"sn\"><p class=\"sn-h\"><span>🔤 Symbols used here</span><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-all\" data-sym=\"*\">📖 All symbols</button></p><ul class=\"sn-l\"><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"partial\">∂</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “partial” (or “del”)</span><span class=\"sn-m\">The partial-derivative sign: keep every other input still, move just one a tiny bit, and see how fast the result changes.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>L</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “L”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the loss: how wrong the model is</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"slash\">/</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “divided by” (slash)</span><span class=\"sn-m\">Another way to write division: 6/3 means 6÷3 = 2.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>W</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “W”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the weights: a table of numbers saying how thick each connection is</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"delta\">δ</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “delta”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the error signal: the partial derivative of the loss with respect to this layer’s output (“how much blame it carries”)</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"mul\">□□</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: no word: side by side means times</span><span class=\"sn-m\">Two things written right next to each other, with no sign between them, are multiplied: a number right before a letter means that number times it; two brackets side by side are multiplied.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>x</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “x”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">this layer’s input</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"T\">□ᵀ</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “transpose”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">Flip a table of numbers over its diagonal: row 1 becomes column 1, row 2 becomes column 2, and so on.</span></li></ul></div><!--/m2pi-->"},"kSCE":{"title":"Softmax + CE gradient p − y","mc":"#15803d","body":"<div class=\"m-meta\"><p class=\"role\">The gradient with respect to the scores <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.044em;\">z</span></span></span></span> is “predicted probabilities − correct answer”: <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_z L=p-y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">∇</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.044em;\">z</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span></span></span></span>.</p><div class=\"where\">📍 Location: <a data-go=\"T1\">App overview</a> › <a data-go=\"T2\">Agent</a> › <a data-go=\"T3\">LLM</a> › <a data-go=\"N\">Neural Network Basics</a> › <a data-go=\"K5\">Math · Derivations &amp; proofs</a> › <b>Softmax + CE gradient p − y</b></div><span class=\"status no\">📐 Math foundation: not a component, but the rule behind the computation</span></div><div class=\"tryit\"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class=\"btn primary\" data-jump=\"labSoftmax\" data-lv=\"K3\">🧪 Softmax and cross-entropy (Probability)</button></div><div class=\"m-tabs\" role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Card sections\"><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t0\" aria-controls=\"mt@p0\" aria-selected=\"true\" tabindex=\"0\">🎯 What it does</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t1\" aria-controls=\"mt@p1\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">▶ How to use</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t2\" aria-controls=\"mt@p2\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">🧠 Why</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t3\" aria-controls=\"mt@p3\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p0\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t0\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"sec\"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>The correct token’s score is pushed up (p − 1 < 0) and the others down (p > 0), each in proportion to its probability.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p1\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t1\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec\" style=\"background:#f0f9ff\"><h4>▶ How to use it</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>It is the starting δ of backpropagation at the output layer.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p2\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t2\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec why\"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>The exponential in softmax and the log in cross-entropy cancel, leaving the simplest possible form.</p></div><div class=\"sec eff\"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Every component lies in (−1, 1), so it is numerically stable.</p></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p3\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t3\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><p class=\"lim\">⚠️ Limits / notes: It assumes the entries of y sum to 1 (one-hot or soft labels).</p></div><!--m2pi--><div class=\"sn\"><p class=\"sn-h\"><span>🔤 Symbols used here</span><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-all\" data-sym=\"*\">📖 All symbols</button></p><ul class=\"sn-l\"><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>z</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “z”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">scores: the raw score the model gives each word</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"nabla\">∇</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “nabla” or “del”: the gradient</span><span class=\"sn-m\">The “gradient”: how fast things change in each input direction, put in a list. It points the steepest way uphill; training takes a small step the opposite way so the loss goes down.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"sub\">□₁</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “sub” (the small lowered mark)</span><span class=\"sn-m\">A small lowered mark is a number or a label that tells apart several things of the same kind: label 1 and label 2 are the 1st one and the 2nd one.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>L</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “L”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the loss: how wrong the model is</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>p</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “p”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the chances that softmax produces</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>y</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “y”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the correct answer’s chances</span></li></ul></div><!--/m2pi-->"},"kSqrt":{"title":"Attention’s √dₖ scaling","mc":"#15803d","body":"<div class=\"m-meta\"><p class=\"role\">Dividing the scores by <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{d_k}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.04em;vertical-align:-0.1828em;\"></span><span class=\"mord sqrt\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8572em;\"><span class=\"svg-align\" style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"padding-left:0.833em;\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-2.8172em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"hide-tail\" style=\"min-width:0.853em;height:1.08em;\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"400em\" height=\"1.08em\" viewBox=\"0 0 400000 1080\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMinYMin slice\"><path d=\"M95,702\nc-2.7,0,-7.17,-2.7,-13.5,-8c-5.8,-5.3,-9.5,-10,-9.5,-14\nc0,-2,0.3,-3.3,1,-4c1.3,-2.7,23.83,-20.7,67.5,-54\nc44.2,-33.3,65.8,-50.3,66.5,-51c1.3,-1.3,3,-2,5,-2c4.7,0,8.7,3.3,12,10\ns173,378,173,378c0.7,0,35.3,-71,104,-213c68.7,-142,137.5,-285,206.5,-429\nc69,-144,104.5,-217.7,106.5,-221\nl0 -0\nc5.3,-9.3,12,-14,20,-14\nH400000v40H845.2724\ns-225.272,467,-225.272,467s-235,486,-235,486c-2.7,4.7,-9,7,-19,7\nc-6,0,-10,-1,-12,-3s-194,-422,-194,-422s-65,47,-65,47z\nM834 80h400000v40h-400000z\"/></svg></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1828em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> brings the dot product’s variance back to 1, so softmax does not saturate.</p><div class=\"where\">📍 Location: <a data-go=\"T1\">App overview</a> › <a data-go=\"T2\">Agent</a> › <a data-go=\"T3\">LLM</a> › <a data-go=\"N\">Neural Network Basics</a> › <a data-go=\"K5\">Math · Derivations &amp; proofs</a> › <b>Attention’s √dₖ scaling</b></div><span class=\"status no\">📐 Math foundation: not a component, but the rule behind the computation</span></div><div class=\"m-tabs\" role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Card sections\"><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t0\" aria-controls=\"mt@p0\" aria-selected=\"true\" tabindex=\"0\">🎯 What it does</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t1\" aria-controls=\"mt@p1\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">▶ How to use</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t2\" aria-controls=\"mt@p2\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">🧠 Why</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t3\" aria-controls=\"mt@p3\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p0\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t0\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"sec\"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>If the components of q and k are independent with mean 0 and variance 1, q·k has variance dₖ (the argument in a footnote of the original Transformer paper).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p1\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t1\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec\" style=\"background:#f0f9ff\"><h4>▶ How to use it</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>With dₖ = 64 the standard deviation is 8; dividing by √64 = 8 brings it back to 1.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p2\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t2\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec why\"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>The more spread out the scores, the closer softmax is to one-hot and the closer the gradient p(1 − p) is to 0.</p></div><div class=\"sec eff\"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>After scaling, attention weights do not lock onto one position at the start of training.</p></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p3\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t3\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><p class=\"lim\">⚠️ Limits / notes: “Independent, variance 1” is a simplifying assumption that explains the magnitude, not an exact description.</p></div><!--m2pi--><div class=\"sn\"><p class=\"sn-h\"><span>🔤 Symbols used here</span><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-all\" data-sym=\"*\">📖 All symbols</button></p><ul class=\"sn-l\"><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"sqrt\">√</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “square root”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">“Which number times itself gives this?”: √9 = 3, because 3×3 = 9.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>d</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “d”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">how many numbers a query or key has (its length)</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"sub\">□₁</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “sub” (the small lowered mark)</span><span class=\"sn-m\">A small lowered mark is a number or a label that tells apart several things of the same kind: label 1 and label 2 are the 1st one and the 2nd one.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>k</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “k”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">at the lower right of d it means “of the keys”</span></li></ul></div><!--/m2pi-->"},"kRes":{"title":"Gradient through residuals","mc":"#15803d","body":"<div class=\"m-meta\"><p class=\"role\">The Jacobian of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=x+F(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> is <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I+\\partial F/\\partial x</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">I</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span></span></span></span>, so the gradient always has a straight-through path.</p><div class=\"where\">📍 Location: <a data-go=\"T1\">App overview</a> › <a data-go=\"T2\">Agent</a> › <a data-go=\"T3\">LLM</a> › <a data-go=\"N\">Neural Network Basics</a> › <a data-go=\"K5\">Math · Derivations &amp; proofs</a> › <b>Gradient through residuals</b></div><span class=\"status no\">📐 Math foundation: not a component, but the rule behind the computation</span></div><div class=\"m-tabs\" role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Card sections\"><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t0\" aria-controls=\"mt@p0\" aria-selected=\"true\" tabindex=\"0\">🎯 What it does</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t1\" aria-controls=\"mt@p1\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">▶ How to use</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t2\" aria-controls=\"mt@p2\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">🧠 Why</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t3\" aria-controls=\"mt@p3\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p0\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t0\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"sec\"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>Even if each layer’s F has a small derivative, the gradient does not multiply away to nothing in a deep network.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p1\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t1\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec\" style=\"background:#f0f9ff\"><h4>▶ How to use it</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>Every Transformer sub-layer is wrapped in a residual: x + attention(x), x + FFN(x).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p2\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t2\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec why\"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Expand the product: ∏(I + Jₗ) = I + ΣJₗ + …; the first term I passes the gradient back unchanged.</p></div><div class=\"sec eff\"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>ResNet (2015) used it to train networks with over a hundred layers.</p></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p3\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t3\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><p class=\"lim\">⚠️ Limits / notes: Residuals solve “gradients cannot get back”, not whether each layer learns something useful.</p></div><!--m2pi--><div class=\"sn\"><p class=\"sn-h\"><span>🔤 Symbols used here</span><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-all\" data-sym=\"*\">📖 All symbols</button></p><ul class=\"sn-l\"><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>y</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “y”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">this layer’s output</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>x</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “x”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the numbers going into this layer</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>F</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “F”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the “change” this layer works out</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"fx\">□( )</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “… of …”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">What comes out when you put the thing in brackets into the “machine” (function) named in front. Like a vending machine: put something in, get something out.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>I</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “I”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the identity matrix: a table that changes nothing, like multiplying by 1</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"partial\">∂</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “partial” (or “del”)</span><span class=\"sn-m\">The partial-derivative sign: keep every other input still, move just one a tiny bit, and see how fast the result changes.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"slash\">/</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “divided by” (slash)</span><span class=\"sn-m\">Another way to write division: 6/3 means 6÷3 = 2.</span></li></ul></div><!--/m2pi-->"},"kPG":{"title":"Policy gradient","mc":"#15803d","body":"<div class=\"m-meta\"><p class=\"role\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta J=\\mathbb{E}\\big[R\\,\\nabla_\\theta\\log\\pi_\\theta(\\tau)\\big]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">∇</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">θ</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0962em;\">J</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.2em;vertical-align:-0.35em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">E</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"delimsizing size1\">[</span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">∇</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">θ</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\">lo<span style=\"margin-right:0.0139em;\">g</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">π</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0359em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">θ</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1132em;\">τ</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"delimsizing size1\">]</span></span></span></span></span>: raise the probability of outputs that earned high reward.</p><div class=\"where\">📍 Location: <a data-go=\"T1\">App overview</a> › <a data-go=\"T2\">Agent</a> › <a data-go=\"T3\">LLM</a> › <a data-go=\"N\">Neural Network Basics</a> › <a data-go=\"K5\">Math · Derivations &amp; proofs</a> › <b>Policy gradient</b></div><span class=\"status no\">📐 Math foundation: not a component, but the rule behind the computation</span></div><div class=\"m-tabs\" role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Card sections\"><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t0\" aria-controls=\"mt@p0\" aria-selected=\"true\" tabindex=\"0\">🎯 What it does</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t1\" aria-controls=\"mt@p1\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">▶ How to use</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t2\" aria-controls=\"mt@p2\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">🧠 Why</button><button type=\"button\" role=\"tab\" id=\"mt@t3\" aria-controls=\"mt@p3\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p0\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t0\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"sec\"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>It gives a gradient even when the reward is not differentiable (e.g. “is the answer right?”).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p1\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t1\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec\" style=\"background:#f0f9ff\"><h4>▶ How to use it</h4><ul class=\"pts\"><li>Sample some answers → score them → update with (R − b)·∇log π, where b is a baseline (e.g. the group’s average score).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p2\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t2\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><div class=\"sec why\"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>The key step is ∇π = π·∇log π (the log-derivative trick), which turns “differentiate a distribution” into “average over samples”.</p></div><div class=\"sec eff\"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Subtracting a baseline leaves the expectation unchanged (proved on this stop) but can cut the variance a lot.</p></div></div><div class=\"m-panel\" id=\"mt@p3\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"mt@t3\" tabindex=\"0\" hidden=\"\"><p class=\"lim\">⚠️ Limits / notes: It is high-variance and needs many samples; it was first set out systematically as REINFORCE (Williams, 1992).</p></div><!--m2pi--><div class=\"sn\"><p class=\"sn-h\"><span>🔤 Symbols used here</span><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-all\" data-sym=\"*\">📖 All symbols</button></p><ul class=\"sn-l\"><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"nabla\">∇</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “nabla” or “del”: the gradient</span><span class=\"sn-m\">The “gradient”: how fast things change in each input direction, put in a list. It points the steepest way uphill; training takes a small step the opposite way so the loss goes down.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"sub\">□₁</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “sub” (the small lowered mark)</span><span class=\"sn-m\">A small lowered mark is a number or a label that tells apart several things of the same kind: label 1 and label 2 are the 1st one and the 2nd one.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"theta\">θ</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “theta”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">all of the model’s “knobs” (parameters) together</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>J</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “J”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the goal: the score you get on average (the expected reward)</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"E\">𝔼</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “expected value”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">“On average, about how much?”: average every outcome, weighted by how likely it is.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"var\"><i>R</i></button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “R”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the reward: the score this answer gets</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"mul\">□□</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: no word: side by side means times</span><span class=\"sn-m\">Two things written right next to each other, with no sign between them, are multiplied: a number right before a letter means that number times it; two brackets side by side are multiplied.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"log\">log</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “log”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">“What power of the base gives this number?”: log₁₀ 100 = 2, because 10² = 100.</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"pi\">π</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “pi”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">the policy: the model itself, giving every possible answer a chance (not the circle number here)</span></li><li><button type=\"button\" class=\"sn-s\" data-sym=\"tau\">τ</button><span class=\"sn-r\">Say: “tau”</span><span class=\"sn-m\">one whole answer: the choices the model makes step by step</span></li></ul></div><!--/m2pi-->"}}
